---
/**
 * The Article 50 notice — one line of fine print at the foot of the page.
 *
 * ── Where it sits, and why that is still compliant ─────────────────────────
 *
 * At the end of `<main>`, immediately above the footer, on every page that
 * carries work made with AI. Owner's instruction, 2026-08-14: smaller, at the
 * bottom, softer.
 *
 * That is not a compromise of Article 50(5). The requirement is that the
 * information reach the person "at the latest at the time of the first
 * interaction or exposure" — it is about WHICH ENCOUNTER, not about which part
 * of the page. The notice is on the same page as the work, present the first
 * time anyone meets it, in the document, in the reader's language. Nothing
 * about it is deferred to a second visit or a second click.
 *
 * And 50(4)'s second subparagraph positively asks for restraint here: on an
 * "evidently artistic, creative, satirical, fictional or analogous work" the
 * obligation is limited to a disclosure "that does not hamper the display or
 * enjoyment of the work". The foot of the page is where fine print belongs; a
 * banner over a card would be less faithful to the Article, not more.
 *
 * ── The floor this cannot go below ─────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Soft is not invisible, and the limit is not a matter of taste:
 *
 *   · `--ink-meta` is the softest ink text may use. `--ink-faint` is documented
 *     in verify:a11y as "never text" — it clears 3:1, which is the bar for
 *     decorative graphics, not the 4.5:1 that meta lines and captions must
 *     meet. Using it here would fail the suite, and it would fail the readers
 *     50(5) names.
 *   · `--t-meta-size` (12px) is already the smallest type in the system. There
 *     is nothing below it and none may be invented — tokens.css is the only
 *     place a raw value may exist.
 *
 * So the softening is done by BREVITY AND PLACEMENT, which cost a reader
 * nothing: one short sentence instead of a paragraph, no bold label, no
 * coloured chip, at the foot instead of under the artwork.
 *
 * ── Machine-readable ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * `digitalSourceType` carries the IPTC controlled URI in microdata, following
 * the pattern `ui/SiteFooter` already uses for schema.org Person. Emitted as
 * `<meta itemprop>` because the value is a URI for a parser, not a sentence
 * for a reader — the reader gets the sentence.
 *
 * BE HONEST ABOUT WHAT THIS IS. The authoritative place for a digital source
 * type is XMP inside the asset, which travels when the file is copied off the
 * page; markup does not. We do not re-encode the artwork, so this is a
 * page-level signal, not a content credential. /ai-transparency says so.
 */
import { aiNoteFor, DIGITAL_SOURCE_TYPE } from '../../config/ai-disclosure';

interface Props {
  /** The page's language. Undefined is English, as everywhere else here. */
  lang?: string;
}

const { lang } = Astro.props;
const note = aiNoteFor(lang);
---

<p class="ai-note t-meta" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/CreativeWork">
  <meta itemprop="digitalSourceType" content={DIGITAL_SOURCE_TYPE.composite} />
  <span itemprop="creditText">{note.text}</span>
  {' '}
  <a class="ai-note__more" href={note.href}>{note.more}</a>
</p>
